    "details": "VulnCheck reports:\n\n> Seven vulnerabilities in phpMyAdmin up to and including 5.2.3 were\n> published on 2026-10-30. The most severe of them, an authentication\n> bypass, is in the WebAuthn second factor: assertion signatures are\n> never verified against the stored public keys, so an attacker who\n> already has the MySQL credentials of a victim can skip the second\n> factor.\n>\n> The others are a server-side request forgery in setup/validate.php,\n> which needs no authentication at all and can be used to probe internal\n> hosts and ports; the removal of two- factor authentication without\n> re-authentication; SQL injection in Central Columns and in the\n> Designer, both running with controluser privileges; stored cross-site\n> scripting in the Designer history; and a missing cross-site request\n> forgery token.\n>\n> There is no fixed release. The phpMyAdmin project has published\n> neither a security announcement nor a new version, and only the\n> request forgery has a fix in the development branch so far.\n",
    "id": "FreeBSD-2026-0572",
